The “entire web” part: how you find any comment mentioning the company

You don’t brute-force scrape the whole web. You run a Company Mention Discovery pipeline:

Step A — Name graph

Collect aliases:

* legal name, brand name, product names
* common misspellings
* old names / acquisitions

Step B — High-leak surfaces first

Priority crawl places where people actually complain:

* Reddit, YouTube comments, app reviews, forums, X replies

Step C — Context gating

A page only counts if the company name appears in experience language, not marketing:

* ✅ “interview at X was chaotic”
* ✅ “X delayed payment”
* ❌ “thanks X for sponsoring”

Step D — Relationship inference (your LinkedIn problem)

When a random person posts about a company, you classify the statement by:

* first-hand vs second-hand vs observational
* specificity (roles, tools, timelines)
* cross-source alignment

So that LinkedIn outsider post becomes:

* L4 (external perception signal) unless it’s clearly first-hand → then it can upgrade to L3/L2.

