Standalone Specification

---

1. Purpose of Layer 6

Layer 6 captures verifiable external events that impose consequences on a company outside its control.

This layer answers:

“What has actually happened to this company in the real world?”

Layer 6 is not opinion, sentiment, or narrative.
It is events, actions, and outcomes.

This layer exists to:

* ground all other layers in reality
* prevent speculation drift
* validate or falsify earlier signals
* provide legal, financial, and operational context

---

2. Canonical definition (LOCKED)

Layer 6 includes publicly documented events, actions, or outcomes involving a company that carry legal, financial, regulatory, or structural consequences.

Key traits:

* externally imposed or externally verified
* documented by third parties
* timestamped
* resistant to narrative manipulation

---

3. What Layer 6 explicitly includes

3.1 Event categories (MANDATORY taxonomy)

Every Layer 6 item must belong to one or more categories.

Column 1	Column 2
Category	Examples
Layoffs & workforce reductions	Layoffs, hiring freezes
Legal actions	Lawsuits, settlements
Regulatory actions	Fines, investigations
Financial distress	Bankruptcy, missed payments
Structural changes	Acquisitions, divestments
Leadership changes	CEO/CFO exits
Compliance failures	Data breaches
Public sanctions	Bans, license loss
Government actions	Audits, penalties


---

3.2 Source categories

Column 1	Column 2	Column 3
Source type	Examples	Priority
Reputable news outlets	Reuters, AP, FT	Very High
Layoff trackers	Layoffs.fyi	High
Court records	Public dockets	High
Business registries	National registries	High
Regulatory bodies	SEC, FTC, EU DPAs	Very High
Financial filings	Annual/quarterly reports	High
Government gazettes	Official notices	Medium


---

4. What Layer 6 explicitly excludes

* Rumors or speculation
* Anonymous tips without verification
* Community discussion (Layer 4)
* Reviews or complaints (Layer 5)
* Opinion pieces without factual basis
* Official company spin unless tied to a verified event

Layer 6 requires independent confirmation.

---

5. Data capture requirements (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

Layer 6 requires precision and traceability.

5.1 Required fields

Column 1	Column 2	Column 3
Field	Required	Notes
Event type	Yes	From taxonomy
Event description	Yes	Factual summary
Event date	Yes	Actual occurrence date
Source	Yes	Third-party authority
Source URL / reference	Yes	Verifiable
Jurisdiction	Yes	Country/state
Severity level	Yes	See below
Confirmation count	Yes	Independent sources


---

5.2 Severity classification

Severity is impact-based, not sensational.

Column 1	Column 2
Level	Definition
Low	Minor, isolated, resolved
Medium	Material but contained
High	Structural or financial impact
Critical	Existential threat


Severity must be defensible, not inflated.

---

6. Platform-by-platform handling

6.1 News media

What to collect

* Headline
* Article body
* Publish date
* Named facts (amounts, counts)
* Company statements (as metadata only)

Rules

* Prefer primary reporting
* Avoid blogs rehashing news
* Cross-check facts across outlets

API vs scraping

* APIs available for some outlets
* Scraping often required
* News aggregators may be used cautiously

---

6.2 Layoff trackers

What to collect

* Layoff date
* Headcount affected
* Percentage of workforce
* Geography
* Source of confirmation

Rules

* Verify against at least one other source
* Track repeated rounds separately

API vs scraping

* Mostly scraping
* Some CSV/feeds available

---

6.3 Court and legal records

What to collect

* Case type
* Filing date
* Parties involved
* Status (filed, ongoing, settled)
* Outcome (if available)

Rules

* Distinguish allegations from judgments
* Track outcomes separately from filings

API vs scraping

* Jurisdiction-dependent
* Many courts require scraping or manual feeds

---

6.4 Business registries

What to collect

* Incorporation changes
* Status changes
* Directors/officers changes
* Dissolutions or insolvency filings

Rules

* Treat registry data as authoritative
* Track changes over time

API vs scraping

* Mixed
* Some registries offer APIs
* Others require scraping

---

6.5 Regulatory bodies

What to collect

* Enforcement action
* Fine amount
* Violated statute
* Resolution date
* Compliance requirements

Rules

* Regulatory findings outweigh media coverage
* Use original authority documents

API vs scraping

